Ad blocking that honours filter options and scales to regional lists.

The shipped parser reads only `$domain=' and `$third-party' and treats every
`||host^' rule as two Lua patterns that are tried against the whole URI of
each request.  That has three consequences once the regional EasyList
variants are added next to EasyList and EasyPrivacy:

 - Rules for the same pattern with different options were de-duplicated by
   pattern alone, so `||googlesyndication.com^$third-party,domain=x' could
   replace the unrestricted rule and let the ad server through everywhere
   else.  Rules are now keyed by pattern and options; a pattern that comes
   with several option sets keeps all of them.

 - Exceptions limited to a resource type (`@@...$script', `$xmlhttprequest')
   were applied to every request, which is how Google Ad Manager requests on
   German news sites slipped past the block list.  The request handler has
   no reliable resource type, so such exceptions are skipped; resource types
   on block rules are ignored, so the rule applies to everything.

 - Every request walked every pattern.  `||host' rules now go into a table
   keyed by host and are matched against the part of the URI after the host;
   the other rules are indexed by their longest literal keyword and only the
   buckets whose keyword occurs in the URI are tried.  Matching 27 lists
   costs about the same per request as the two default lists did before,
   and the rule tables sent to each web process are smaller.

`$domain=' now also covers the subdomains of the listed domain, and
`$third-party' compares registrable sites rather than exact host names.

Element hiding rules (`##') remain unsupported.

